<div><h3>I. Introduction</h3><p>The Earth Observation Satellite (EOS) [1] serves as a pivotal platform for acquiring space-based information. It provides extensive coverage, prolonged observation duration, freedom from borders and airspace constraints, and eliminates the need to consider personnel safety [2], [3], [4]. Consequently, it actively contributes to land surveying, topographic exploration, disaster warning, and military intelligence acquisition [5]. However, the continual evolution of satellite technology and increasingly intricate imaging tasks have presented more formidable challenges for satellite mission planning in recent years.</p></div>